[Unit]
Description=token-watch published summary (the ONLY file the tracking page reads)
Documentation=file:///opt/token-watch/summary.py

# ⛔⛔ THIS UNIT DID NOT EXIST UNTIL 2026-09-09, AND ITS ABSENCE WAS INVISIBLE FROM EVERY
#    ANGLE EXCEPT THE PAGE ITSELF. summary.py has always had a __main__ that publishes
#    atomically; nothing ever ran it on a schedule. The file was written once by hand on
#    2026-09-08 07:01 and then sat there. So every component looked healthy and the page
#    went stale.
# ⛔ ONE WRITER (B-TOKEN-WATCH-FOLLOWUP-MEMORY P6(d), pre-audit A5): summary.build() both
#    advances the fold's cursors and publishes the file, and the follow-up calls it inside
#    its own lock; summary.py's main() now builds only under the store's periodic lock and
#    SKIPS when another job holds it. (This header used to say the follow-up only folded the
#    numbers into its own stats and never wrote the published file; at this ref build()
#    writes it, and the pre-audit's A5 is the record of the two writers.)
#    ★ Found by Coltrane and Langston independently, on their first-ever look at the page,
#      because the page's own staleness badge said so and nobody had been looking.
# B-TOKEN-WATCH-STORAGE OBJ-4 (#1132): a failure pages instead of passing silently.
OnFailure=token-watch-failed@%n.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
User=tokenwatch
Group=tokenwatch
ExecStart=/usr/bin/python3 /opt/token-watch/summary.py
# ⛔ A MEMORY CAP, SET FROM ITS OWN MEASURED PEAK (B-TOKEN-WATCH-FOLLOWUP-MEMORY P3) — RESTORED AT P12, IN THE SAME
#    INSTALL THAT PLACES THE CODE IT WAS MEASURED ON (Langston, P3 Step 4 C-4). The reconcile of the box's 2026-09-02
#    summary.py, recorded: it equals its blob at the previous install 5e6f1cec once carriage returns are stripped
#    (sha256 2b6f2b73…, same for budget.py and promote.py), so the installer's guard passes and nothing hand-written
#    is overwritten unseen. The installer refuses a unit whose MemoryMax= is new or changed when its script is not
#    placed by that run; P12's CORE places summary.py.
MemoryMax=384M
#    P7 (2026-11-15, staging, read-only, committed code, writes redirected): a summary build from an EMPTY state —
#    births, tombstones, the 1.8 GB observation fold and the 5,000-member watch set — 64.3 s, 176 MiB peak, after
#    summary._read_new was streamed (it was 1,915 MiB from empty: the 258 MB tombstone file read whole).
#    Measured 2026-11-15 on staging, summary.build with the P2b-2 code (/opt/token-watch-p1 = 9aebff599) over a copy of
#    the scratch store (its dead.jsonl byte-identical to the live one, 258,564,193 B): under MemoryMax=512M ru_maxrss
#    175,228 KiB (171 MiB), 6.8 s; re-run AT THE CAP TO SHIP, a fresh copy ~03:05Z: MemoryMax=384M -> 7.1 s, 175,336
#    KiB, success; the same copy again under 512M -> 6.9 s, 175,080 KiB. (systemd's own "Memory peak" reads 1.6M for
#    these units; ru_maxrss is the kernel's high-water.) 384M is ~2.2x the peak and under 15% of the box's available
#    memory (2,648 of 3,814 MB). It never runs beside the follow-up's 512M: both take the periodic lock and this unit
#    skips when it is held.
#    ⚠️ HEADROOM IS SPENT IN MONTHS (Langston): the dead-set build dominates (+158 MiB for 766,794 deaths), so 384M
#    binds near ~1.8M dead records, ~3 months at the observed death rate. summary.py logs its peak every run and exits 3
#    (OnFailure pages) past 70% of its cgroup's cap; raising or removing the cap is a P10/P15 item of the batch.
# ⚠️ Nothing here touches the trading app. It reads the study's own store and writes ONE
#    derived file into the published directory — the isolation summary.py's own docstring
#    describes ("the study publishes one derived file and the app reads that").
